<title>GERMANY: German builder says private funds may create jobs.</title>
<headline>German builder says private funds may create jobs.</headline>
<dateline>ESSEN, Germany 1996-12-22</dateline>
<text>
<p>German builder Hochtief AG at the weekend suggested that public construction projects, including motorways, should be financed privately as this could create new jobs.</p>
<p>Hans-Peter Keitel, management board chairman of Hochtief, told German newspaper Westdeutsche Allgemeine Zeitung that private financings would ensure that construction can be started immediately, something which would help create jobs.</p>
<p>Currently strained public coffers could prompt the government to postpone such projects indefinitely, Keitel said.</p>
<p>&quot;If motorways, universities and prisons were built privately and financed through rents, then some 170,000 new jobs could be created,&quot; Keitel said.</p>
<p>German Transport Minister Matthias Wissmann is said to be leaning towards such financings despite heavy objections to the plans from bureaucrats.</p>
<p>-- Frankfurt Newsroom, +49 69 756525</p>
